1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:03,400
Life below the sea.

2
00:00:06,280 --> 00:00:09,220
It's never been safe for submariners.

3
00:00:11,880 --> 00:00:13,000
Fire.

4
00:00:13,740 --> 00:00:14,440
Flood.

5
00:00:14,780 --> 00:00:15,700
Explosions.

6
00:00:16,580 --> 00:00:17,700
Lives snuffed out.

7
00:00:19,020 --> 00:00:19,720
Thresher.

8
00:00:19,960 --> 00:00:20,660
Scorpion.

9
00:00:21,280 --> 00:00:21,980
Course.

10
00:00:22,660 --> 00:00:24,960
Catastrophic events seared into memory.

11
00:00:26,040 --> 00:00:26,920
Hard lessons.

12
00:00:26,921 --> 00:00:32,040
Now, Sub Disasters on Modern Marvels

13
00:00:32,041 --> 00:00:46,041
No matter how calm the waters may be on the surface

14
00:00:51,800 --> 00:00:53,000
Down below

15
00:00:53,001 --> 00:00:55,960
With awesome power and silent fury

16
00:00:56,860 --> 00:01:00,400
They can destroy the submariner and his steel vessel in a heartbeat.

17
00:01:03,180 --> 00:01:07,140
It's been said the sea is a more hostile environment than space.

18
00:01:08,540 --> 00:01:12,120
Submarines operate in an inherently dangerous environment.

19
00:01:12,121 --> 00:01:20,260
They operate deep under the ocean, under tremendous pressures from the compressed ocean water.

20
00:01:20,261 --> 00:01:26,980
and any mistake, any technical malfunction can potentially be fatal.

21
00:01:27,360 --> 00:01:29,180
You can't bail out as you might in an airplane.

22
00:01:29,720 --> 00:01:31,600
You can't lower the boats as you might on the surface.

23
00:01:32,320 --> 00:01:36,220
You can't jump off the deck of a carrier and hope to survive and get picked up.

24
00:01:37,220 --> 00:01:38,540
There's no place to go.

25
00:01:38,541 --> 00:01:52,541
Any system malfunction can potentially lead to a loss of power.

26
00:01:54,620 --> 00:01:58,420
If this occurs, the ship loses its maneuverability,

27
00:01:59,060 --> 00:02:01,220
its ability to generate breathable air,

28
00:02:01,480 --> 00:02:03,760
and ultimately, its ability to service.

29
00:02:08,540 --> 00:02:11,660
Out of power, out of control.

30
00:02:12,180 --> 00:02:15,580
In a worst-case scenario, it sinks to its crush depth,

31
00:02:16,040 --> 00:02:19,500
where the structure can no longer withstand the increasing water pressure.

32
00:02:22,260 --> 00:02:24,800
An entire crew perishes.

33
00:02:27,800 --> 00:02:31,400
But this triggers a vital response within the submarine community.

34
00:02:31,760 --> 00:02:35,360
The basic cycle goes that first we find out that something's going wrong.

35
00:02:35,680 --> 00:02:38,220
Then there's an investigation. We try to figure out why it went wrong.

36
00:02:38,540 --> 00:02:41,960
The investigation will lead to some sort of findings as to the root cause,

37
00:02:42,180 --> 00:02:45,280
and that gives us an idea as to what we need to do to try to make things better.

38
00:02:50,700 --> 00:02:54,660
In 1968, mindful of successes and failures,

39
00:02:55,000 --> 00:02:58,980
the U.S. Navy was moving ahead with its nuclear submarine program.

40
00:02:58,981 --> 00:03:11,600
Five years earlier, it had suffered the loss of Thresher with all 129 men aboard.

41
00:03:13,660 --> 00:03:20,260
But after an investigation, it had implemented changes aimed at advancing the cause of submarine safety.

42
00:03:20,261 --> 00:03:31,640
on may 21st 1968 all was well with the scorpion a nuclear sub with 99 men aboard

43
00:03:31,641 --> 00:03:37,280
the fast attack sub had left the mediterranean after a successful mission shadowing the soviet

44
00:03:37,281 --> 00:03:42,700
fleet it radioed its position about 50 miles south of the azores

45
00:03:42,701 --> 00:03:53,360
but on may 22nd it lay in pieces on the bottom of the ocean under 10 000 feet of water its crew of

46
00:03:53,361 --> 00:03:59,580
99 all dead the loss of the scorpion was a huge shock because it did come after all these

47
00:03:59,581 --> 00:04:01,840
improvements had been made after the thresher was lost

48
00:04:01,841 --> 00:04:10,940
the story of the scorpion is one of those painful episodes that defies the notion of closure

49
00:04:10,941 --> 00:04:16,100
many questions few answers

50
00:04:16,101 --> 00:04:23,340
there's no way of finding out what the crew was going through in the last moments of their lives

51
00:04:23,341 --> 00:04:32,040
they don't have cockpit voice recorders in submarines there's no really there's no way to

52
00:04:32,041 --> 00:04:38,940
know that the scorpion was not in contact with people at the time that it sank there have been

53
00:04:38,941 --> 00:04:44,760
rumors ranging from Russian submarines torpedoing it to a battery well explosion to a hot run in

54
00:04:44,761 --> 00:04:52,701
the torpedo tube that caused the torpedo to go off prematurely.

55
00:04:52,702 --> 00:04:56,860
Two decades later, the Navy still

56
00:04:56,861 --> 00:05:00,584
wanted to know what had happened to its men and their sub.

57
00:05:00,585 --> 00:05:02,960
It built Jason Jr., a deep-sea robot,

58
00:05:03,360 --> 00:05:05,499
which was sent down to investigate.

59
00:05:05,500 --> 00:05:08,800
And sure enough, there was an explosion in the forward

60
00:05:08,801 --> 00:05:11,137
torpedo room.

61
00:05:11,138 --> 00:05:24,800
While some believe the Soviet sunk Scorpion in retaliation for one of theirs

62
00:05:24,801 --> 00:05:30,180
we may have sunk, the official Navy theory focuses on a faulty battery in the compartment

63
00:05:30,181 --> 00:05:32,709
next to the torpedo room.

64
00:05:32,710 --> 00:05:38,780
A now declassified Navy report from 1987 speculates that the

65
00:05:38,800 --> 00:05:41,560
that a battery explosion fits the damage to the submarine.

66
00:05:44,360 --> 00:05:47,760
There is a tremendous amount of energy stored in a submarine battery,

67
00:05:47,860 --> 00:05:50,240
even a small one like what we had on nuclear power boats,

68
00:05:50,780 --> 00:05:52,980
that could cause a fairly serious explosion.

69
00:05:54,540 --> 00:05:57,580
That could have triggered a subsequent explosion in the torpedo room,

70
00:05:57,760 --> 00:06:00,060
which would have blown a huge hole in Scorpion.

71
00:06:00,660 --> 00:06:03,880
It would have flooded rapidly and then sunk to its crush depth.

72
00:06:04,520 --> 00:06:08,300
When you puncture the hull of a submarine at that kind of a depth,

73
00:06:08,800 --> 00:06:12,460
the rapid change in air pressure causes the air to ignite,

74
00:06:12,820 --> 00:06:14,300
and essentially snuffs everybody aboard.

75
00:06:19,260 --> 00:06:21,720
But at 10,000 feet on the ocean bottom,

76
00:06:22,560 --> 00:06:26,120
the details of Scorpion's demise may forever remain a mystery.

77
00:06:27,360 --> 00:06:29,740
You know, you can't go down there and look at it.

78
00:06:29,741 --> 00:06:32,360
You can run a submersible down there and take pictures,

79
00:06:32,560 --> 00:06:37,000
but you can't actually go down and touch it like you could an air crash, for example.

80
00:06:37,001 --> 00:06:41,580
So any submarine sinking, there's always a great deal of mystery around it.

81
00:06:49,360 --> 00:06:56,900
Nonetheless, the Navy forged ahead, acting on the most plausible theory explaining the Scorpion's untimely end.

82
00:06:58,280 --> 00:07:00,500
About a year after this submarine disaster,

83
00:07:01,400 --> 00:07:06,320
Naval Ordnance ordered a redesign and replacement of the torpedo battery that had been red flagged.

84
00:07:07,000 --> 00:07:14,520
So whether that was the cause or not, the search for an answer led to an improved technology that made subs even safer.

85
00:07:15,660 --> 00:07:17,100
Dive! Dive!

86
00:07:21,040 --> 00:07:21,740
Dive!

87
00:07:21,800 --> 00:07:26,898
Since the Scorpion submarine tragedy, there have been no subs lost by the U.S.

88
00:07:26,899 --> 00:07:28,140
in nearly 40 years.

89
00:07:30,720 --> 00:07:33,920
But that wasn't the case in the beginning of submarine history.

90
00:07:33,921 --> 00:07:40,280
The first three submarines enlisted into military service all encountered trouble.

91
00:07:42,240 --> 00:07:48,780
During the American Revolution, George Washington's forces included a one-man submarine called the Turtle.

92
00:07:49,800 --> 00:07:53,640
Its mission was to attach an explosive to a British warship in New York Harbor.

93
00:07:54,920 --> 00:07:58,180
It failed, although the submariner survived.

94
00:07:58,181 --> 00:08:06,680
in the american civil war submarines were once again called into service

95
00:08:06,681 --> 00:08:13,640
the union navy developed a sub called the alligator but it never saw active duty as it

96
00:08:13,641 --> 00:08:21,360
was lost in transport during a storm but by far the civil war's most compelling submarine story

97
00:08:21,361 --> 00:08:23,660
centers on a Confederate vessel.

98
00:08:24,960 --> 00:08:27,860
On a chilly February night in 1864,

99
00:08:28,580 --> 00:08:31,700
eight men squeezed on board the CSS H.L. Hunley

100
00:08:31,701 --> 00:08:35,460
for a mission below the waters of Charleston Bay, South Carolina.

101
00:08:37,600 --> 00:08:41,600
Essentially, it was a pipe about 40 feet long,

102
00:08:42,640 --> 00:08:47,260
and the crew, the eight unfortunates

103
00:08:47,261 --> 00:08:49,480
who made up the motive power of the thing,

104
00:08:49,481 --> 00:08:54,840
sat on a bench and operated a crank which turned the propeller.

105
00:08:56,580 --> 00:09:00,680
Despite its Spartan accommodations, the Hunley was advanced for its time.

106
00:09:01,720 --> 00:09:06,540
It had a rudimentary bellow system for its snorkel, diving planes, and ballast tanks.

107
00:09:12,980 --> 00:09:16,980
The mission of the Hunley and its crew was to sink the USS Housatonic

108
00:09:16,981 --> 00:09:20,260
by attaching its spar torpedo to the ship's hull.

109
00:09:22,540 --> 00:09:25,880
The torpedo was attached to the end of the spar,

110
00:09:26,660 --> 00:09:30,740
and it was a copper canister filled with 75 pounds of black powder.

111
00:09:31,420 --> 00:09:35,100
And it was attached to a barb, much like a thimble.

112
00:09:35,460 --> 00:09:39,540
So when they ran the barb into the ship and they pulled back,

113
00:09:40,020 --> 00:09:43,460
the canister would remain and then the bar would pull out.

114
00:09:44,100 --> 00:09:46,040
And so they would go back 100 yards,

115
00:09:46,041 --> 00:09:48,820
and then there was a reel on the Hunley.

116
00:09:48,920 --> 00:09:52,680
And when they got to the end of the 100 yards, that would ignite the powder.

117
00:09:54,620 --> 00:09:56,700
That's exactly what the Hunley's crew did.

118
00:09:57,220 --> 00:10:02,040
And when the explosion occurred, just as planned, the Housatonic began to sink.

119
00:10:02,940 --> 00:10:06,940
The Hunley had pulled off the world's first successful submarine combat mission.

120
00:10:07,920 --> 00:10:12,280
This much we know, but the rest of the story was incomplete for over a century.

121
00:10:12,520 --> 00:10:15,760
Well, the mystery was that the Hunley didn't return to port.

122
00:10:16,040 --> 00:10:18,100
And so what happened to the Hunley?

123
00:10:19,840 --> 00:10:24,260
The next day, the Confederates could see the mass of the Housatonic sticking out of the water,

124
00:10:24,340 --> 00:10:25,840
but the Hunley never came back.

125
00:10:29,040 --> 00:10:33,800
Remarkably, after a 15-year quest, the Hunley was discovered in 1995

126
00:10:33,801 --> 00:10:39,120
by a team of underwater archaeologists under the direction of author Clive Kessler.

127
00:10:46,040 --> 00:11:00,040
A special harness was designed and built for the delicate process of raising and investigating this amazing piece of history.

128
00:11:10,800 --> 00:11:14,760
And it was about a thousand feet off the Housatani.

129
00:11:16,040 --> 00:11:21,820
And what it turned out to be that it was waiting for the tide to turn,

130
00:11:21,940 --> 00:11:24,660
because it didn't sink, obviously, from the explosion.

131
00:11:25,800 --> 00:11:31,460
And I believe that the Canandaigua, which was a ship coming to rescue the Housatonic survivors

132
00:11:31,461 --> 00:11:35,260
who were up in the rigging, struck the Hunley and sank it.

133
00:11:36,080 --> 00:11:39,980
The fate of the Hunley showed that the technology was still too rudimentary

134
00:11:39,981 --> 00:11:42,500
and unsafe for widespread practical use.

135
00:11:42,501 --> 00:11:45,620
with only man-powered propulsion

136
00:11:45,621 --> 00:11:49,160
the crew of the Hunley couldn't get away in time to survive

137
00:11:49,161 --> 00:11:53,280
four decades later

138
00:11:53,281 --> 00:11:56,580
an inventor and entrepreneur named John Holland

139
00:11:56,581 --> 00:11:59,340
developed a practical vessel for the U.S. Navy

140
00:11:59,341 --> 00:12:02,780
that was ultimately known as the forerunner of the modern submarine

141
00:12:02,781 --> 00:12:05,660
at first steam engines were tried

142
00:12:05,661 --> 00:12:08,400
while powerful they had a drawback

143
00:12:08,401 --> 00:12:14,740
The negative was that, of course, the thing became like a blast furnace.

144
00:12:14,741 --> 00:12:18,820
It was so hot, and the crew suffered accordingly.

145
00:12:20,400 --> 00:12:26,424
After five prototypes, Holland built the USS Holland, a six-man sub for the U.S.

146
00:12:26,425 --> 00:12:27,125
Navy.

147
00:12:29,320 --> 00:12:36,640
It featured a dual-engine system, gasoline power for surface cruising, and an electric motor for submerged travel.

148
00:12:38,400 --> 00:12:39,860
But these had inherent dangers.

149
00:12:40,580 --> 00:12:43,360
For one thing, the battery for the electric motor could explode.

150
00:12:44,220 --> 00:12:48,460
And if seawater got into the battery, it would produce deadly chlorine gas,

151
00:12:48,560 --> 00:12:52,080
the same kind of gas used in World War I in the trenches to kill enemy soldiers.

152
00:12:53,460 --> 00:12:56,100
But it wasn't just the batteries that posed a danger.

153
00:12:57,540 --> 00:13:01,020
Fumes from the internal combustion engines could also cause explosions.

154
00:13:01,021 --> 00:13:08,620
gasoline explosions accounted for a number of submarines sinking in the early years of the 20th century

155
00:13:08,621 --> 00:13:18,400
to deal with this submarine engineers switched to diesel diesel is not highly volatile diesel is

156
00:13:18,401 --> 00:13:27,080
relatively hard to ignite so it's a fairly safe fuel and much much safer than than gasoline still

157
00:13:27,081 --> 00:13:31,140
there were many submarine disasters in the early years of the 20th century.

158
00:13:32,280 --> 00:13:35,760
Fire, flood and collisions all resulted in loss of lives.

159
00:13:36,780 --> 00:13:40,260
When mishaps occurred, there was no rescue technology in place.

160
00:13:41,240 --> 00:13:44,220
That would come only after two prominent disasters,

161
00:13:44,780 --> 00:13:48,640
inspired of visionary commander named Charles Momsen.

162
00:13:52,160 --> 00:13:54,680
When the USS Scorpion went down,

163
00:13:54,681 --> 00:14:00,120
It was armed with 23 torpedoes, two of them nuclear-tipped.

164
00:14:01,700 --> 00:14:04,880
Sub Disasters will return on Modern Marvels.

165
00:14:09,220 --> 00:14:13,060
We now return to Sub Disasters on Modern Marvels.

166
00:14:14,640 --> 00:14:21,460
On September 25, 1925, an American submarine, the USS S-51,

167
00:14:21,461 --> 00:14:24,820
collided with a cargo ship off the coast of Rhode Island

168
00:14:24,821 --> 00:14:28,480
and went down in 130 feet of water.

169
00:14:31,760 --> 00:14:35,660
Commander Charles Swede Momsen was ordered to take his sub,

170
00:14:35,920 --> 00:14:39,720
the USS S-1, out to search for the crippled vessel.

171
00:14:42,880 --> 00:14:46,320
When he found it, he soon realized there were survivors.

172
00:14:47,180 --> 00:14:48,740
He knew there were people alive in there.

173
00:14:48,840 --> 00:14:49,820
They were banging on the hull.

174
00:14:50,220 --> 00:14:50,920
You could hear them.

175
00:14:50,921 --> 00:14:52,960
And there wasn't a thing he could do for them.

176
00:14:53,420 --> 00:14:55,240
And there wasn't a thing they could do for themselves.

177
00:14:55,380 --> 00:14:56,920
They had no way of getting out the boat.

178
00:15:05,720 --> 00:15:10,380
Two years later, when a second sub, the USS S-4, was lost,

179
00:15:11,500 --> 00:15:13,160
Momsen was spurred to take action.

180
00:15:13,660 --> 00:15:16,640
He sees the inadequacy of the measures taken,

181
00:15:17,020 --> 00:15:19,700
and so he decides that they need more sophisticated equipment,

182
00:15:19,701 --> 00:15:25,880
and he goes on to invent two types of devices that will be very important to the saving of submarine crews in the future.

183
00:15:27,240 --> 00:15:32,620
In 1929, he first tested what was to become the Momsen lung.

184
00:15:33,640 --> 00:15:39,160
Momsen's device was an oblong rubber bag with two tubes that recycled exhaled air.

185
00:15:40,040 --> 00:15:47,320
The lung contained a canister of soda lime, which removed carbon dioxide from exhaled air and then replenished it with oxygen.

186
00:15:49,700 --> 00:15:53,720
Soon, sailors were using the Momsen Lung in disaster simulation training,

187
00:15:54,040 --> 00:15:56,300
in which they learned to escape from a submarine.

188
00:15:57,060 --> 00:16:02,440
The original simulation device was the tower that let submariners practice

189
00:16:02,441 --> 00:16:07,380
entering the water from a depth of from 50 to 100 to 150 feet,

190
00:16:07,880 --> 00:16:13,080
and to feel what it was like, and to experience what it's like to use this breathing device,

191
00:16:13,081 --> 00:16:17,020
the Momsen Lung, as they ascended from the depths to the surface.

192
00:16:19,700 --> 00:16:25,360
In 1929, the Navy then pushed for the development of a rescue chamber.

193
00:16:26,260 --> 00:16:31,260
Begun by Momsen and later modified by a colleague, Lieutenant Commander Alan McCann,

194
00:16:31,820 --> 00:16:34,980
the Navy built a large, steel, bell-shaped chamber,

195
00:16:35,460 --> 00:16:39,040
which could be lowered with cables down to the escape hatch on a sunken submarine.

196
00:16:39,980 --> 00:16:47,560
A watertight seal to the submarine could be created by placing a rubber gasket around the diving bell's bottom and reducing the air pressure.

197
00:16:49,700 --> 00:16:53,440
The hatch could then be opened, and the trapped submariners could climb aboard.

198
00:16:54,340 --> 00:16:59,880
A hatch on the top of the bell would allow the rescued submariners and diving bell operators an easy way out.

199
00:16:59,881 --> 00:17:06,940
They were trying to perfect ways of getting out of the submarine, but it hadn't been tried in an emergency situation.

200
00:17:08,220 --> 00:17:12,560
It was only a matter of time before Momsen's equipment would be put to the test.

201
00:17:13,560 --> 00:17:18,920
In 1939, the United States Navy launched its newest submarine, the USS Squalus.

202
00:17:19,700 --> 00:17:23,950
But it had new technology. It was the latest thing.

203
00:17:23,951 --> 00:17:26,700
And not everything was perfected.

204
00:17:26,701 --> 00:17:38,700
In its very first year, the USS Squalus had been refitted and was carrying out her post-refit trials off the coast of Portsmouth, New Hampshire.

205
00:17:38,701 --> 00:17:48,700
Its problems began with a malfunction of the main induction valve, a 36-inch opening in the hull that provided air needed for the operation of the diesel engines.

206
00:17:48,701 --> 00:17:53,960
Just before diving, the indicator showed that it was closed and safe to submerge.

207
00:17:54,460 --> 00:17:58,720
The valve was open, and that flooded out the engine room.

208
00:18:01,320 --> 00:18:06,760
And it happened so quickly that there was no way they could counter the effect of the inrush of water.

209
00:18:08,460 --> 00:18:12,180
The sub was down 243 feet below the surface.

210
00:18:13,560 --> 00:18:16,000
28 men died almost immediately.

211
00:18:16,520 --> 00:18:19,000
33 members of the crew, however, do survive.

212
00:18:20,560 --> 00:18:22,240
They're in a watertight compartment,

213
00:18:22,620 --> 00:18:27,420
and they release a small float with a radio telephone that floats to the surface.

214
00:18:30,100 --> 00:18:34,920
The captain of the Suelis tells the people on the surface that his crew survives

215
00:18:34,921 --> 00:18:36,220
and that they need rescue.

216
00:18:36,420 --> 00:18:37,440
Then the phone line is cut.

217
00:18:39,580 --> 00:18:43,420
So these men in these three compartments were still alive.

218
00:18:43,421 --> 00:18:47,700
They were far below anything that any man had ever been rescued from before.

219
00:18:48,280 --> 00:18:52,440
They spread soda lime out on the decks to absorb CO2 in the air.

220
00:18:53,480 --> 00:19:00,660
The men not actually required to do something were told to go lie down, conserve energy and conserve oxygen.

221
00:19:02,060 --> 00:19:04,100
And they waited for rescue to come.

222
00:19:06,640 --> 00:19:11,780
When the Navy finally realized what had happened, six agonizing hours later,

223
00:19:12,780 --> 00:19:17,700
A rescue party was organized under the guidance of none other than Charles Swede Momsen,

224
00:19:18,120 --> 00:19:20,020
who was flown in from Washington, D.C.

225
00:19:20,800 --> 00:19:23,240
His invention, the McCann Rescue Chamber,

226
00:19:23,820 --> 00:19:27,480
was to be utilized in an attempt to save all the surviving submariners.

227
00:19:29,140 --> 00:19:31,640
There were three compartments with air in them,

228
00:19:31,860 --> 00:19:34,500
but the middle compartment was the forward battery compartment.

229
00:19:35,080 --> 00:19:38,480
They were afraid that seawater would get into the battery and produce poison gas,

230
00:19:38,820 --> 00:19:40,520
so the men sealed that off.

231
00:19:40,521 --> 00:19:45,200
So there was the control room with half the men in it, then this empty space,

232
00:19:45,420 --> 00:19:48,520
and then the forward torpedo room with the other half of the men.

233
00:19:51,580 --> 00:19:55,380
Eventually, the worst-case scenario occurred in the forward battery compartment.

234
00:19:56,920 --> 00:20:02,460
As water seeped into the squalas, it mixed with the acid in the batteries and produced chlorine gas.

235
00:20:04,260 --> 00:20:07,440
But they used the Mopsin lung as a gas mask, and it was very effective.

236
00:20:10,520 --> 00:20:17,500
Meanwhile, on the surface, the Navy had gotten its ships, men, and equipment ready for the rescue.

237
00:20:19,040 --> 00:20:26,800
They were then able to hover the diving bell down and seal it against the hull and begin to carry the men up.

238
00:20:27,680 --> 00:20:31,180
Initially, they thought it would take five trips to bring all the men to the surface.

239
00:20:32,520 --> 00:20:36,220
But something told Momsen, don't play it safe.

240
00:20:37,220 --> 00:20:41,740
So he had them overload the bell and get them up in four trips instead of five.

241
00:20:44,620 --> 00:20:48,620
On the fourth trip, the cable bringing them up broke.

242
00:20:49,620 --> 00:20:51,160
The bell was cast loose.

243
00:20:54,120 --> 00:20:56,280
It had one thin strand left.

244
00:20:59,080 --> 00:21:03,640
They got 50 or so crewmen to hold the cable and pull it up by hand.

245
00:21:06,220 --> 00:21:11,840
If he'd gone with five missions instead of four,

246
00:21:12,240 --> 00:21:15,320
the last group of men would have been left trapped on the bottom with no way out.

247
00:21:19,580 --> 00:21:22,060
It's a spectacular success for the U.S. Navy,

248
00:21:22,061 --> 00:21:24,680
and it's a true testament to the U.S.

249
00:21:24,681 --> 00:21:28,080
Navy's valuable investment in rescue technology.

250
00:21:29,920 --> 00:21:34,000
All the equipment was right there on hand to be used,

251
00:21:34,001 --> 00:21:36,520
and it had been developed and was ready to go.

252
00:21:37,140 --> 00:21:40,880
And the experienced personnel were available to make it work.

253
00:21:43,840 --> 00:21:46,820
The rescue was also a victory for Charles Momsen.

254
00:21:48,040 --> 00:21:49,700
Momsen was an innovator.

255
00:21:50,540 --> 00:21:52,020
He was a true believer.

256
00:21:52,760 --> 00:21:55,120
He was the kind of person who makes a real difference.

257
00:21:55,900 --> 00:21:59,160
When he came along, nobody had ever been rescued from a sunken sub.

258
00:21:59,580 --> 00:22:00,800
Nobody thought it was possible.

259
00:22:01,120 --> 00:22:02,820
And he said, I'm going to change that.

260
00:22:02,960 --> 00:22:03,660
And he did.

261
00:22:04,000 --> 00:22:11,000
Eventually the squalus was raised, refloated, repaired and recommissioned as the USS Sailfish.

262
00:22:13,000 --> 00:22:20,000
Improvements in submarine systems ensured that the malfunctions that initially put the squalus at risk would not reoccur.

263
00:22:21,000 --> 00:22:25,000
But the world's superpowers would soon be involved in a Cold War

264
00:22:26,000 --> 00:22:30,000
that would push submarine technology beyond any previous limits of safety.

265
00:22:34,000 --> 00:22:36,360
The squaless could travel 20 knots on the surface.

266
00:22:37,020 --> 00:22:37,960
Nine knots submerged.

267
00:22:38,900 --> 00:22:42,180
She'd made 18 successful test dives on the day she sank.

268
00:22:43,400 --> 00:22:46,160
Sub Disasters will return on Modern Marvels.

269
00:22:49,100 --> 00:22:52,940
We now return to Sub Disasters on Modern Marvels.

270
00:22:52,941 --> 00:23:04,380
During the Cold War, the United States and the Soviet Union were engaged in an escalating and frightening arms race.

271
00:23:06,960 --> 00:23:08,720
It was the nuclear age.

272
00:23:09,360 --> 00:23:15,600
And during the 1960s, both sides seemed on the brink of annihilating one another, if not the world.

273
00:23:18,000 --> 00:23:21,920
This was the same era as the Cuban Missile Crisis, the Berlin Crisis.

274
00:23:21,921 --> 00:23:25,040
people thought there was going to be a nuclear war with Russia,

275
00:23:25,320 --> 00:23:28,560
and every new piece of military technology was vital.

276
00:23:29,220 --> 00:23:32,420
What this meant was that things were rushed into service real quick.

277
00:23:35,000 --> 00:23:39,280
And that included submarines, which were also entering the nuclear age.

278
00:23:40,120 --> 00:23:42,320
A nuclear-powered sub was more efficient

279
00:23:42,321 --> 00:23:45,020
and could remain below the water's surface longer

280
00:23:45,021 --> 00:23:48,780
and therefore remain undetected for longer periods of time.

281
00:23:48,781 --> 00:23:55,400
With a nuclear plant, your submarine could go as long as the groceries last.

282
00:23:56,400 --> 00:23:57,700
That's your only limitation.

283
00:23:58,660 --> 00:24:00,100
That could be up to six months.

284
00:24:01,320 --> 00:24:03,980
And it stays tight. And it's fast.

285
00:24:04,940 --> 00:24:09,860
It ended up to a war machine that could secretly get closer to enemy territory than ever before.

286
00:24:10,400 --> 00:24:13,760
And then launch its missiles, if it came to that.

287
00:24:13,761 --> 00:24:21,400
So when submarines switched to nuclear power plants, as with the Nautilus, and soon afterwards

288
00:24:21,401 --> 00:24:33,760
started carrying nuclear weapons, the magnitude of potential disasters increased exponentially.

289
00:24:33,761 --> 00:24:35,980
It's been a deadly game.

290
00:24:35,981 --> 00:24:41,300
The Russians have lost five nuclear subs, and the U.S. has lost two.

291
00:24:41,301 --> 00:24:53,400
In every case, all men aboard have perished.

292
00:24:53,401 --> 00:24:59,960
The story of the U.S. Navy's Thresher is a painful chapter from the Cold War era.

293
00:24:59,961 --> 00:25:07,800
The fast attack sub was the 15th nuclear sub launched and the lead ship of the Permit class.

294
00:25:07,801 --> 00:25:13,720
Only in the morning of April 10th, 1963, the sub was on a test dive off the coast of Cape Cod,

295
00:25:14,380 --> 00:25:16,920
accompanied by the U.S. rescue ship Skylark.

296
00:25:18,560 --> 00:25:24,380
It was going to what's called test depth, which is the maximum safe depth that a submarine can sail at.

297
00:25:25,760 --> 00:25:30,040
And it was in constant contact with the surface as it went through this dive.

298
00:25:32,680 --> 00:25:36,880
But at one point, Thresher signaled a message that it was having minor problems.

299
00:25:37,800 --> 00:25:40,320
This would be the last contact with the sub.

300
00:25:40,720 --> 00:25:44,400
My guess would be that the people on board were working through these things,

301
00:25:44,460 --> 00:25:45,920
that there was probably not a lot of panic,

302
00:25:46,500 --> 00:25:49,060
that they were just trying harder and harder to save things

303
00:25:49,061 --> 00:25:51,260
until they realized that they couldn't.

304
00:25:53,640 --> 00:25:57,280
The Navy would later conclude that the trouble began the previous summer

305
00:25:57,281 --> 00:26:02,880
when Thresher had suffered some damage to 28 joints on its piping during shock testing.

306
00:26:02,881 --> 00:26:09,580
others speculated that an earlier collision with a tugboat was another possible culprit

307
00:26:09,581 --> 00:26:17,360
in either case during thresher's dive to test depth a joint on a small pipe began to let water in

308
00:26:17,361 --> 00:26:23,160
which immediately shorted out some electrical controls which controlled the nuclear reactor

309
00:26:23,161 --> 00:26:27,260
and when that shorted out the reactor shut down

310
00:26:27,261 --> 00:26:34,400
Out of power, the sub could no longer communicate with rescue ship Skylark.

311
00:26:35,080 --> 00:26:37,040
At this point, it began to sink.

312
00:26:37,420 --> 00:26:38,660
Submarines work like airplanes.

313
00:26:38,780 --> 00:26:41,360
As they move through the water, they have a certain buoyancy.

314
00:26:41,460 --> 00:26:42,380
It helps them keep up.

315
00:26:42,860 --> 00:26:44,780
When they lost power, it started sinking.

316
00:26:45,240 --> 00:26:48,440
And it was already at test depth, the maximum safe depth.

317
00:26:53,600 --> 00:26:56,720
The emergency blow system could have helped them to surface.

318
00:26:57,260 --> 00:26:58,760
But it too failed.

319
00:26:59,580 --> 00:27:03,620
This is a process in which compressed air is directed to different sections of the sub

320
00:27:03,621 --> 00:27:06,040
in order to control surfacing and diving.

321
00:27:06,300 --> 00:27:09,060
The way that the emergency blow system was set up then

322
00:27:09,061 --> 00:27:12,000
was going through relatively narrow pipes with a lot of turns

323
00:27:12,001 --> 00:27:15,620
that went through pressure-reducing valves, and the system froze up.

324
00:27:15,680 --> 00:27:18,940
So they couldn't get high-pressure air into the ballast tanks to blow the water out,

325
00:27:19,240 --> 00:27:20,980
and they had no way to get to the surface.

326
00:27:25,040 --> 00:27:26,980
The men must have been aware of trouble.

327
00:27:27,260 --> 00:27:29,620
but just kept trying to save the vessel.

328
00:27:30,320 --> 00:27:31,500
They knew something was happening.

329
00:27:31,660 --> 00:27:33,460
They probably didn't know what it was exactly.

330
00:27:34,580 --> 00:27:37,380
They were probably doing their jobs as they were trained to do them.

331
00:27:38,680 --> 00:27:41,580
But before the men could restore the electrical circuits,

332
00:27:42,360 --> 00:27:43,860
thresher sank to crush depth.

333
00:27:44,340 --> 00:27:47,760
When they get to crush depth, what happens is the sea pressure

334
00:27:47,761 --> 00:27:50,600
so quickly crushes the submarine.

335
00:27:50,601 --> 00:27:51,820
It gives way all at once.

336
00:27:52,460 --> 00:27:55,520
The air is compressed so quickly and so intensely

337
00:27:55,521 --> 00:27:57,300
that it bursts into flames.

338
00:27:58,040 --> 00:28:00,460
So the men on those submarines didn't die of drowning.

339
00:28:00,720 --> 00:28:01,500
They were burned to death.

340
00:28:01,640 --> 00:28:03,980
In fact, they were incinerated in a burst of light.

341
00:28:09,560 --> 00:28:11,720
129 men lost their lives that morning.

342
00:28:12,440 --> 00:28:16,580
They went down with their sub to the ocean bottom at some 8,000 feet.

343
00:28:16,581 --> 00:28:19,340
In the submarine community, it was a shock.

344
00:28:19,880 --> 00:28:25,480
It was a shock quite different from the shock it presented to the families and relatives.

345
00:28:25,520 --> 00:28:28,680
of the lost crewmen, it shouldn't have happened.

346
00:28:29,840 --> 00:28:32,780
It's the sort of thing that's not supposed to happen.

347
00:28:33,300 --> 00:28:34,720
We found out that we weren't perfect,

348
00:28:35,020 --> 00:28:37,820
that we hadn't learned as much about submarine design as we thought,

349
00:28:37,980 --> 00:28:40,080
and that we could still lose even the newest boats.

350
00:28:43,840 --> 00:28:46,700
Thresher's nuclear reactor was not damaged in the disaster

351
00:28:46,701 --> 00:28:49,320
and remains intact at the bottom of the sea.

352
00:28:50,260 --> 00:28:52,380
Furthermore, there are no lost nuclear weapons,

353
00:28:52,381 --> 00:28:54,560
as she was on a test mission and not armed.

354
00:28:55,520 --> 00:29:01,060
The Thresher tragedy led to a number of changes aimed towards averting disaster.

355
00:29:01,960 --> 00:29:05,320
The implementation of better safety standards in nuclear subs,

356
00:29:06,040 --> 00:29:08,940
a means of restarting the nuclear reactor in case of emergency,

357
00:29:09,600 --> 00:29:12,140
and a redesign of the ballast blow tank system.

358
00:29:12,660 --> 00:29:15,820
So you had large diameter pipes that did not reduce the pressure,

359
00:29:16,220 --> 00:29:19,360
that just blew high pressure air into the ballast tanks to blow the water out

360
00:29:19,361 --> 00:29:20,880
to get you up to the surface in a hurry.

361
00:29:20,881 --> 00:29:29,580
when thresher plunged its sailors to the subs crush depth there was as yet no technology to

362
00:29:29,581 --> 00:29:34,460
rescue a downed sub that was far below sea level but that hadn't reached its breaking point

363
00:29:34,461 --> 00:29:42,200
the deep submergence rescue vehicles or dsrvs were created as a direct result of the loss of

364
00:29:42,201 --> 00:29:49,060
thresher in april of 1963 the navy realized that even if thresher had gone down in an area of the

365
00:29:49,061 --> 00:29:51,565
ocean which would permit access to her.

366
00:29:51,566 --> 00:29:54,520
We did not have the technology to go down that

367
00:29:54,521 --> 00:29:57,980
far, even that far, and bring the crew back to the surface to safety.

368
00:29:59,760 --> 00:30:04,056
The U.S. Navy developed electrically powered DSRVs.

369
00:30:04,057 --> 00:30:06,920
These can rescue up to 24 men at a

370
00:30:06,921 --> 00:30:07,621
time.

371
00:30:07,622 --> 00:30:17,200
Loaded up on a C-5 transport plane, they can get to a sub in distress in any ocean

372
00:30:17,201 --> 00:30:18,500
in a matter of hours.

373
00:30:19,560 --> 00:30:22,060
In fact, they can even be piggybacked on another submarine.

374
00:30:22,240 --> 00:30:25,880
They have cradles made for them that allow them to be piggybacked on another submarine.

375
00:30:30,840 --> 00:30:36,500
Once the DSRV locates the disabled sub, it uses its special docking system.

376
00:30:37,080 --> 00:30:42,340
The black and white ring seen here mates with the upper escape trunk hatch of the sub in distress.

377
00:30:42,341 --> 00:30:50,260
Then the men are taken out of the sub in groups and brought by the DSRV to another sub or a surface vessel.

378
00:30:52,720 --> 00:31:01,800
Unfortunately, in the year 2000, even DSRVs would be too little too late for the men aboard the Russian submarine, Kursk.

379
00:31:01,801 --> 00:31:11,080
The thresher broke up into six major sections

380
00:31:11,081 --> 00:31:14,340
with debris scattered over 400 yards square.

381
00:31:16,160 --> 00:31:19,220
Sub Disasters will return on Modern Marvels.

382
00:31:23,280 --> 00:31:27,020
We now return to Sub Disasters on Modern Marvels.

383
00:31:27,021 --> 00:31:32,180
In over 40 years of involvement with nuclear submarines,

384
00:31:32,780 --> 00:31:36,840
the Russians suffered the loss of five vessels and hundreds of lives.

385
00:31:39,520 --> 00:31:42,200
While the U.S. certainly made its share of mistakes,

386
00:31:42,660 --> 00:31:45,680
it hasn't lost a nuclear sub in nearly 40 years.

387
00:31:46,600 --> 00:31:47,780
So why the disparity?

388
00:31:48,360 --> 00:31:50,740
Communism did not reward people for doing good work,

389
00:31:50,900 --> 00:31:53,180
as a result of which there wasn't any point in doing good work,

390
00:31:53,740 --> 00:31:56,060
and as a result of that, they got a lot of bad work.

391
00:31:57,020 --> 00:31:59,940
They're theoretical. People were world class. They still are.

392
00:32:00,900 --> 00:32:04,500
But unless you can tighten the nut on a bolt, you don't have things break.

393
00:32:06,300 --> 00:32:08,348
During the Cold War, both the U.S.

394
00:32:08,349 --> 00:32:11,600
and Soviets were hurrying new technology into service.

395
00:32:12,300 --> 00:32:15,300
The U.S. paid the price with the losses of Thresher and Scorpion.

396
00:32:16,000 --> 00:32:18,540
And the Soviets suffered in this deadly game as well.

397
00:32:18,660 --> 00:32:21,900
They were rushing to catch up, and they cut corners.

398
00:32:22,560 --> 00:32:27,600
In addition, because of the Soviet environment, decisions were made politically.

399
00:32:27,920 --> 00:32:30,840
Mistakes could mean the end of a career, even the end of a life.

400
00:32:31,000 --> 00:32:32,720
So people did not report mistakes.

401
00:32:34,380 --> 00:32:40,980
And it bit them hard in engineering that they couldn't produce quality goods to deal with a specific task.

402
00:32:41,820 --> 00:32:43,740
And that cost a lot of lives for the Soviets.

403
00:32:43,741 --> 00:32:52,340
Today, the Kursk has the painful distinction of being Russia's most famous lost nuclear sub.

404
00:32:57,880 --> 00:33:01,560
The tragic events began August 12, 2000.

405
00:33:01,880 --> 00:33:05,180
Kursk was on exercise in the Barents Sea, and she disappeared.

406
00:33:05,181 --> 00:33:09,920
she had been scheduled to fire practice torpedoes

407
00:33:09,921 --> 00:33:14,180
at the Russian service ships as an exercise.

408
00:33:17,960 --> 00:33:20,680
Our hydrophones and any submarines we had in the area

409
00:33:20,681 --> 00:33:21,920
picked up a big explosion,

410
00:33:22,860 --> 00:33:26,360
and then the Russians started looking for a lost submarine,

411
00:33:26,880 --> 00:33:28,200
and that was really all that we knew.

412
00:33:30,480 --> 00:33:34,400
A second explosion registered a 3.5 on the Richter scale.

413
00:33:35,180 --> 00:33:38,840
the equivalent of the detonation of 1.5 tons of explosives.

414
00:33:41,820 --> 00:33:45,760
The Russians knew this unfolding disaster hadn't gone unnoticed,

415
00:33:46,140 --> 00:33:50,640
but didn't alert world media until nearly 48 hours after the event.

416
00:33:50,980 --> 00:33:53,040
They were not very forthcoming right off the bat,

417
00:33:53,920 --> 00:33:55,360
which is understandable.

418
00:33:55,500 --> 00:33:57,200
It's embarrassing to lose a nuclear submarine.

419
00:33:58,340 --> 00:33:59,800
A Russian admiral went on television,

420
00:34:00,560 --> 00:34:03,920
declared that Kursk had suffered technical failures,

421
00:34:03,921 --> 00:34:09,640
had sunk and that in all likelihood there were no survivors.

422
00:34:11,660 --> 00:34:18,480
While there were 118 men aboard, there were in fact 23 survivors, desperate to be rescued.

423
00:34:21,540 --> 00:34:27,160
The Kursk was in shallow waters, only 354 feet below the surface.

424
00:34:27,161 --> 00:34:37,900
immediately other nations including the u.s and great britain offered help but the russians

425
00:34:37,901 --> 00:34:46,000
rejected any assistance they would do it on their own i don't think that there was ever any chance

426
00:34:46,001 --> 00:34:50,660
of those men being rescued the russians were far too slow getting their resources on the sea

427
00:34:50,661 --> 00:35:02,740
Had everybody had the right equipment online and ready to go at that very moment,

428
00:35:02,840 --> 00:35:04,300
maybe somebody might have survived.

429
00:35:05,340 --> 00:35:07,420
That requires about four consecutive miracles.

430
00:35:08,320 --> 00:35:10,800
And emergencies happen in an unexpected time.

431
00:35:10,860 --> 00:35:11,840
That's why they're called emergencies.

432
00:35:12,200 --> 00:35:16,560
In October 2001, the Russians, aided by the Dutch,

433
00:35:17,040 --> 00:35:20,080
razed the Korsk and removed her nuclear reactor and weapons.

434
00:35:20,660 --> 00:35:23,080
What caused the sinking of the Korsk?

435
00:35:23,680 --> 00:35:29,040
A Russian commission concluded the most likely cause for the explosion was a collision with another sub.

436
00:35:29,680 --> 00:35:31,260
They based this on two facts.

437
00:35:31,740 --> 00:35:35,080
First, the long and deep scratch marks along the side of Korsk.

438
00:35:35,920 --> 00:35:40,300
Secondly, the USS Memphis was tracked in the area at the time of the disaster,

439
00:35:40,860 --> 00:35:45,320
and shortly afterwards docked in nearby Norwegian waters for emergency repairs,

440
00:35:45,920 --> 00:35:48,440
the nature of which has never been disclosed.

441
00:35:50,660 --> 00:36:00,560
While this theory is tantalizingly plausible,

442
00:36:01,360 --> 00:36:03,220
the U.S. categorically rejects it

443
00:36:03,221 --> 00:36:07,100
and insists the Russians were using a dangerously volatile fuel.

444
00:36:08,100 --> 00:36:11,000
What appears to have sunk the Kursk

445
00:36:11,001 --> 00:36:17,100
was a defective torpedo fueled with HTP fuel, high-test peroxide.

446
00:36:17,101 --> 00:36:21,980
It would appear that this torpedo was damaged

447
00:36:21,981 --> 00:36:28,000
to the degree that some of the fuel leaked out of the torpedo onto the deck.

448
00:36:28,660 --> 00:36:33,120
When HTP gets on steel and oil, it explodes.

449
00:36:35,040 --> 00:36:38,960
What really caused this disaster will probably never be ascertained.

450
00:36:39,300 --> 00:36:40,960
But the bottom line is this.

451
00:36:41,580 --> 00:36:44,200
Had those sailors had equipment to help them breathe,

452
00:36:44,201 --> 00:36:47,540
They could have possibly survived until rescue crews got to them.

453
00:36:47,880 --> 00:36:51,760
They had atmosphere cleansing equipment back there of some kind,

454
00:36:52,560 --> 00:36:59,000
the Russian equivalent of an oxygen generator and a CO2 absorption unit to clean the atmosphere.

455
00:36:59,680 --> 00:37:04,320
It seems that in an attempt to get this equipment going, they started a small fire,

456
00:37:04,740 --> 00:37:09,820
which depleted the oxygen in the compartment and rendered the machinery useless.

457
00:37:09,821 --> 00:37:14,200
So they were, regrettably, they were pretty well doomed.

458
00:37:23,820 --> 00:37:33,260
To this day, six nuclear submarines, four Russian and two American, remain on the ocean bottom.

459
00:37:34,260 --> 00:37:38,560
They have nuclear power plants and an estimated 44 nuclear warheads,

460
00:37:39,180 --> 00:37:41,300
some on torpedoes, some on missiles.

461
00:37:46,060 --> 00:37:50,520
Navy officials and nuclear scientists claim there is little to be concerned about.

462
00:37:51,420 --> 00:37:54,080
The chance of an explosion is nearly impossible,

463
00:37:54,740 --> 00:37:57,720
and environmental contamination is also of little concern.

